						<td style="font-size: 11pt;"> 
							 
							<br>
							<br>
							06:55 AM EST March 27, 2003 
							<br>
							The Associated Press<br><br><br>EL PASO, Texas<p class="nitfp">It was Pfc. Howard Johnson Jr.'s first trip out of the country, part of a Fort Bliss-based unit that wasn't expected to see combat.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The 21-year-old from Mobile, Ala., is one of two soldiers confirmed dead Wednesday from the Army's 507th Maintenance Company. Also killed in action was Spc. Jamaal R. Addison, 22, of Roswell, Ga.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"We had no idea war would break out and we would lose our son," said Howard Johnson's mother, Gloria Johnson, as tears streamed down her face.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Two days before the war began, Addison had spoken with his mother and relatives in Covington, Ga.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"He didn't want to get off the telephone, he just wanted to hold on to that conversation as long as possible," said family pastor, the Rev. Julius Kidd.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Other families of soldiers from the 507th continue to wait for more information. Five members of the unit are prisoners of war, shown in Iraqi television video. Eight others are missing after they were ambushed on Sunday when a convoy took a wrong turn near An Nasiriyah in central Iraq.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Ruben Estrella is the father of Pvt. Ruben Estrella-Soto, 18, who also is listed as missing in action. The El Paso resident said Fort Bliss officials told him about his son's status on Sunday.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"It's bad. We don't know anything, not knowing anything," Estrella said in Spanish.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Pfc. Jessica D. Lynch, 19, of Palestine, W.Va., also missing in action, joined the Army as a way of financing her goal of becoming an elementary teacher. Jobs are scarce in rural Wirt County, where January's unemployment rate was 15 percent.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"She was excited to go," said her father, Gregory Lynch. "If I could talk to her I would say, 'Don't be scared because there's people out there looking for you right now.'"</p>
<p class="nitfp">In the West Texas town of Pecos, a car with U.S. Army markings rolled through carrying news to the family of a missing soldier that his name would soon be released to the media.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"We're in a small town and when people see an official vehicle, they think they'll find out the worst," said Javier Contreras, a cousin of Chief Warrant Officer Johnny Villareal Mata, 35, who is listed as missing in action.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Another Army soldier from the 507th missing in Iraq is Master Sgt. Robert J. Dowdy, 38, of Cleveland. He is two years from retirement.</p>
<p class="nitfp">His brother, Army Reserve Master Sgt. Jack Dowdy Jr., 40, in Kaneohe, Hawaii, described him as athletic and a dedicated military man. "Wherever they sent him he automatically went."</p>
<p class="nitfp">Others listed as missing in action are Spc. James M. Kiehl, 22, of Comfort; Pfc. Lori Ann Piestewa, 23, of Tuba City, Ariz.; Pvt. Brandon Ulysses Sloan, 19, of Bedford, Ohio; and Sgt. Donald Ralph Walters, 33, of Salem, Ore.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Five members of the 507th are prisoners of war. They are Spc. Edgar Hernandez, 21, of Mission; Spc. Joseph Neal Hudson, 23, of Alamogordo, N.M.; Spc. Shoshana Nyree Johnson, 30, of El Paso; Pfc. Patrick Wayne Miller, 23, of Walter, Kan.; and Sgt. James Joseph Riley, 31, of Pennsauken, N.J.</p>
<p class="nitfp">U.S. Rep. Silvestre Reyes said on Wednesday that the 507th Maintenance Company ran into a heavily armed Iraqi combat unit that included two tanks and automatic weapons when it made the wrong turn near An Nasiriyah.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Reyes, D-El Paso, a member of the House Armed Services Committee, said he is angry that the company was not protected or escorted by a combat company when it was attacked by Iraqi forces.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Reyes, who said he has spoken to high ranking Department of Defense officials, said the lightly armed 507th didn't have a chance.</p>
<p class="nitfp">A Department of Defense official said he could not comment on Reyes' statements, but that the incident was still under investigation.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Reyes said he has contacted Pentagon officials and is awaiting answers concerning the ambush.</p>
<p class="nitfp">About 4,500 soldiers have deployed from Fort Bliss to the Middle East and about 100 to 150 of those are with the 507th.</p>

